\f1\fs22 He\'92d meant the bit about the Elvines. He had phone numbers for days. He scrolled his tablet through them, thumb hovering, and found his urge sinking. What he wanted, under the hurt, with a feast abandoned and the bidet playlist still primed to romance mode, was to be somewhere a woman couldn\'92t walk out of. Somewhere that had never once made him feel like the wrong man standing in the right room. All the wealth in the world, clearly, could not grant him that.\
He wanted his movie.\
\'93Fisher,\'94 he said, to the room, because the room had staff in it now whether he could see them or not. \'93Do you know how to play a film?\'94\
Fisher arrived from a fold in the air the way he always did. \'93The residence maintains a private cinema on the mezzanine, sir. Forty-two seats.\'94\
\'93Forty-two?\'94\
\'93Mr Hardy disliked sitting near other people.\'94 The lopsided ELVIS smile surfaced and submerged. \'93And, latterly, near himself.\'94\
Chanta thought he could empathise with that; right then, he didn\'92t want to sit with himself either. \'93I only need the one.\'94\
\'93Which title did sir have in mind?\'94\
Chanta told him. Fisher did not blink, having been trained not to, but something crossed the velvet of him anyway\'97the small professional grief of a man asked to pour a vintage into a tin mug. \'93
\f2\i Harum Scarum
\f1\i0 , sir. A bold selection.\'94\
\'93It\'92s the best one.\'94\
\'93It is widely regarded to be the worst one, sir.\'94\
\'93Yeah.\'94 Chanta nodded, content the way he was content about squalor. \'93Same thing.\'94\
He ordered a cheeseburger, fries, and\'97because the Wine Elvis had once recommended it\'97a Kenyan lager. The burger was perfect. The fries were perfect. The lager tasted wrong, and it took him half the opening titles to place why: they must have brewed it with clean water. You could drink the whole bottle and your guts wouldn\'92t even file a complaint. He missed the complaint. He drank it anyway, the way you finish a conversation with someone who won\'92t stop talking over you.\
Then the lights went down in a room the size of a chapel, and Johnny Tyronne fell off a movie poster into a desert kingdom.\
Chanta watched the way he always watched: the same way he catalogued beautiful, office-bred Airborne women. Aishah appeared on screen first, all kohl and trouble\'97
\f2\i Biotech, 
\f1\i0 Chanta decided, with those come-tax-me eyes, lab coat or no. A girl in the back row of the harem: 
\f2\i Finance
\f1\i0 , obviously. Then the Shalimar, the slave girl, came down the palace stair and, before she reached the bottom, had passed one guard a note, tipped something into a goblet, and given a third man a look that left him visibly reconsidering his loyalties.\
\'93Hello, princess,\'94 Chanta said to the movie screen. 
\f2\i Events Management
\f1\i0 , surely, he thought\'97a woman who could organise the fall of a kingdom and never once clean her own toilet bowl. 
\f2\i Maybe they didn\'92t have toilet bowls in Zanzibar
\f1\i0 , Chanta thought
\f2\i , or whatever country that is
\f1\i0 . Then Shalimar opened her mouth and out came a magnolia drawl, soft and Southern on a desert princess, and that was 
\f2\i Publishing
\f1\i0  to the bone, the endearing little catch a voice only earned in Publishing. 
\f2\i Events Management.
\f1\i0  No. 
\f2\i Publishing.
\f1\i0  He\'92d settle it before the credits.\
Then Leilah appeared, blonde and harem-veiled, and his heart did a disloyal little skip, the one it saved for one filing only: 
\f2\i Cyber Security
\f1\i0 . For half a second Leilah was Sarah, who actually was Cyber Security, who\'92d kicked his heart into arrhythmia and walked out of a real penthouse an hour or so ago without looking back. Then Sarah was an actor in a film older than Ulyssia, and Chanta\'92s want had nowhere to go but into the movie. He ate a chip. On screen, Johnny Tyronne sang to eleven women at once and meant it about none of them, and Chanta felt the disreputable warmth of being told a lie he\'92d bought and still wanted.\
By the time the credits climbed, he\'92d decided. A man who owned more money than he could spend, and more singing bidets than he\'92d ever use (so far, he\'92d used precisely zero)\'85 What was it he\'92d decided again? \'93Fisher. I\'92ve never in my whole life seen a Harum Scarum series Elvis. Isn\'92t that odd?\'94\
\'93It\'92s understandable, sir.\'94 A pause with weather in it. \'93Very few were cloned to that age and appearance. Fewer still were ever sold. The market found them\'85\'94 Fisher selected the kindest available term and held it at arm\'92s length. \'93\'85
\f2\i niche
\f1\i0 , sir.\'94\
\'93So they\'92d be a collector\'92s item, right?\'94\
\'93I doubt it, sir. 
\f2\i Harum Scarum 
\f1\i0 was regarded to be the\'97\'94\
\'93\'97best Elvis movie ever made.\'94\
\'93Yes, sir. Your taste is truly individual.\'94\
Chanta liked that Fisher considered him truly individual.\
\'93Fisher, have we revived all of Hardy\'92s Elvi?\'94\
Fisher paused. \'93Yes, sir. All of the staff have been revived and are happily going about their business.\'94\
\'93That\'92s good,\'94 Chanta said. \'93But \'85 are there others?\'94\
\'93Others, sir?\'94\
\'93You said, \'91staff\'92. All of the \'91staff\'92 have been revived.\'94\
Fisher said nothing.\
\'93Did Hardy own Elvi who were not \'91staff\'92?\'94\
Fisher exhaled. It was the most emotive thing he\'92d done since Chanta had known him. \'93The estate retains a quantity of mint condition stock in bunker storage, sir.\'94\
Chanta jumped to his feet. \'93Fisher, I wanted all the Elvi awake. I wanted that two weeks ago. Every Elvis has a right to a life.\'94\
The white gloves found one another. \'93Onboarding them all at once would be a cruelty, sir. A blank clone requires orientation\'85 a back story, if you like, where they fit in society, their vocation\'85\'94\
\'93Like a script,\'94 Chanta said.\
\'93I suppose, but without plot holes, or at least no holes you\'92d want the Elvis to fill out in random ways.\'94\
Chanta thought on that. \'93They\'92d be like babies?\'94\
Fisher grappled to find words. \'93More like children, I suppose\'85 no, that\'92s not it either.\'94\
\'93What was it like when you were taken out of the crate, Fisher?\'94\
\'93None of us recalls, sir. But I do remember having the skills I needed to do my job the moment I needed to.\'94\
\'93And you could always sing.\'94\
\'93Yes, sir.\'94\
\'93And swing your hips.\'94\
\'93Perhaps not as well as others, sir. A billionaire\'92s Steward has little need for hip swinging.\'94\
Chanta thought. \'93We need a plan, Fisher. I want all of them alive and well, do you understand?\'94\
\'93Yes, sir. I will call a meeting with the staff.\'94\
\'93Good. Now \'85 if there\'92s any Harum Scarum series down there, I want one of them revived first.\'94\
