\f1\fs22 \'93Provisioning?\'94 Chanta guessed. The lift accelerated down, and his feet almost floated out of his shoes.\
Chanta had never seen an orchestra on stage in his life, but he imagined the lift could hold the whole shebang, including the harps, drums, grand pianos, violins, banjos and pedal steel guitars. He\'92d never played tennis, but he\'92d seen where a court used to be in the Ghetto, overgrown by weeds both obnoxious and smokable; he imagined how many grand pianos would fit on it and decided the service lift was bigger.\
The lift seemed to go down deeper than Chanta\'92s whole previous life had gone up. Chanta could have drained a king-sized Fried-Banana Br\'fbl\'e9e Latte by the time the lift slowed and the doors opened like the curtains in an expensive movie theatre.\
Chanta stepped out, into a space that was less movie theatre and more football stadium. It was a cavern. He clapped his hands and was disappointed no echo returned. He sang a couple of lines of a tune he was workshopping; they died in the industrial-clean air as if they were sucked straight from his lungs. Where Chanta expected to find a store room or a large stasis locker, he stumbled into a mash-up of warehouse, carpark, cathedral, and a military depot that comes with its own weather.\
Among sports cars, jeeps, bikes, forklifts and a small knocked-up boat that must have had only sentimental value, Chanta walked towards aisles of racks the height of the tenement he grew up in, and in better nick. He passed a fleet of vintage limousines sitting nose-to-tail under transparent dust sheets, plates never issued, odometers still on zero, as if in a showroom for an afterlife nobody had come to collect. Beyond them, a hangar of things he couldn\'92t name and a wall of things he could: toilet paper enough to apologise to every Cloudborne for the persistent shortages and ensuing supermarket riots; soap in quantities that implied a stain no washing had yet dissolved; and ammunition enough to wage a war against New Sheepland or ungrateful guests.\
Chanta walked the first aisle the way you\'92d walk into a church you\'92d been excommunicated from because you thought the hymns were cheesy. After two more aisles, Chanta didn\'92t know where to head other than with his next footstep, and so he wandered, got lost, found his way, got lost again, retraced steps and circled round until he had walked far enough to be in another postcode. \
With no sense of a route back to the service lift he came upon an aisle where stasis lockers the size of his old apartment block stood humming in rows. One contained a whole harvest of bananas held at the precise green instant before ripening. Another was full of cucumbers and eggplants. Another contained sides of beasts hanging from hooks. Another hung plucked birds of such dizzying sizes and shapes that Chanta wondered if he\'92d find a tweetie bird or a road runner. One locker contained pizzas of only one variety: ham and pineapple.\
\'93Savages,\'94 Chanta muttered.\
He found no Elvi in that aisle, which possibly just meant that Hardy had no intention of cooking any of them.\
Chanta realised it could take him weeks to search the whole place\'97he should have just asked Fisher to show him where the bodies were rested rather than sneak down here in the middle of the night. Giving up, he tried to retrace his steps, but after only a few aisles, he was someplace else altogether.\
Chanta had been lost before, admittedly in parking stations or suburbs smaller than this cavern, so he resorted to a plan that had always worked: try going in the wrong direction until he landed somewhere he definitely recognised. He might have spent more of his life wandering around parking stations than he would have liked, but he was still here to tell the tale.\
And he was still lost.\
He pulled his tablet out of his pocket and was about to call Fisher for help, but was too stubborn and embarrassed to confess his predicament. He jammed the tablet back in his pocket, then, out of frustration, yanked it out again and shouted right into where he thought the microphone was. \'93Hey, stupid! Tell me where the Elvi are stored, tax it!\'94\
The tablet played a quick joyful melody that reminded him of a poker machine announcing a modest payout. On the screen, a spinner animated then dissolved, leaving a map zooming in so fast Chanta thought it was a racing car game. The image rotated and plotted a green line from the bottom of the screen to the top right. An arrow popped up with the words: 
\fs20 YOU ARE HERE.\
